  "diagnosis": "The issue is that AbortSignal listeners are not being properly cleaned up after tasks complete. The current implementation adds a listener in #throwOnAbort but doesn't remove it when the task finishes successfully, is rejected, times out, or is aborted. This causes memory leaks when using shared AbortSignals.",
  "hypothesis": "We need to ensure that AbortSignal listeners are removed in all code paths - successful completion, rejection, timeout, and abortion - by storing the listener reference and calling removeEventListener appropriately.",
  "reason": "The current #throwOnAbort method creates a one-time listener but doesn't provide a mechanism to clean it up when the task completes through other means. This leads to accumulating listeners on shared AbortSignals.",
